mod std.io.fiber;

/**
 * lock_stripes - a small, fixed set of lock objects shared by all the short
 * lived synchronisation objects of the library (jobs, deferreds, futures, ...).
 *
 * USAGE
 *     class job {
 *         private lk : object = lock_stripes.next();    // picked once, never changes
 *         def is_active() : bool { lock(lk) { return state == 1; } }
 *     }
 *
 * WHY NOT lock(self)
 * The runtime keeps one entry for every object that was ever used in a
 * lock() statement, in ONE linked list, and every lock and unlock walks that
 * list to find its entry. A program that starts 10,000 coroutines and locks
 * each job once would make that list 10,000 long and every lock in the whole
 * program 10,000 steps slow. With stripes the list stays at STRIPE_COUNT
 * entries however many jobs exist. Objects that share a stripe only share the
 * lock, not their data; the critical sections are a few field reads and writes,
 * so the extra contention is small.
 *
 * C++ / Java equivalent: lock striping, e.g. the segments of the old
 * java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap or Guava's Striped<Lock>.
 *
 * THE RULE THAT COMES WITH IT
 *   Never take a second library lock while holding a stripe.
 *   Two different objects may have been given the SAME stripe, and the runtime
 *   lock is not counted: lock(a) { lock(a) { } ...here the lock is already
 *   released... }. A nested lock on the same stripe would silently end the
 *   outer critical section early. Every class that uses a stripe states in its
 *   own comment that it follows this rule.
 *
 * LOCKING DISCIPLINE
 *   stripes  is filled once by setup(), which the start-up code runs on the
 *            main thread before any other thread or fiber exists
 *            (std.io.coroutines start_scheduler(), called by platform.srt_init),
 *            and is only read afterwards.
 *   cursor   is advanced without a lock on purpose: two fibers that race only
 *            get the same stripe, which is harmless.
 *
 * Nothing here suspends.
 */
class lock_stripes {

    /** how many lock objects there are */
    private static const STRIPE_COUNT : decimal = 64;

    private static stripes : object[]?;
    private static cursor : decimal = 0;

    /**
     * Create the lock objects. Safe to call more than once. Must run for the
     * first time while the program still has a single fiber (see above).
     */
    static def setup() {
        if(stripes == null) {
            created := new object[64];
            for(i : decimal = 0; i < 64; i++) {
                created[i] = new _object_();
            }

            stripes = created;
        }
    }

    /**
     * The lock object for a new synchronisation object: the stripes are handed
     * out in turn. Keep the result in a field; do not call next() per lock.
     */
    static def next() : object {
        if(stripes == null) {
            setup();
        }

        cursor++;
        if(cursor >= 64) {
            cursor = 0;
        }

        // a racing fiber may have moved the cursor between the two statements above
        index : decimal = cursor;
        if(index < 0 || index >= 64) {
            index = 0;
        }

        return stripes[index];
    }
}
